<?xml version="1.0" encoding="UTF-8"?>
<!--
  THE PLUGIN BROWSER'S FORMAT FILTER (QBX-78), AND THE BUILT-INS IT
  NEVER HIDES (QBX-261, phases 2, 5 and 9 to 12).

  One product is legitimately installed in several formats — that is why the
  browser is a tree with a Format column and why a selection resolves by
  (format, uid) rather than by name (proposal 08 M2). A user who has decided
  which format they work in had no way to say so: this case gates the combo
  that lets them, and the fact that the choice is REMEMBERED.

  WHY THE PLUGIN LIST IS SUPPLIED HERE. `assert-plugin-browser` normally reads
  the real registry, which holds what is INSTALLED ON THE MACHINE: four plugins
  in two formats on one dev box, the two linked-in `tw` built-ins alone on a CI
  runner, and whatever a developer has in ~/.vst3 anywhere else. A format
  filter cannot be distinguished from a no-op unless at least two formats are
  present, so every claim below would be vacuous on the runner that matters.
  `plugins=` hands the dialog a literal list instead, replacing its one
  registry read and nothing else. Phase 0 is the one assertion made over the
  REAL registry, and it is deliberately weak: the two built-ins are the only
  plugins every build is guaranteed to have.

  THE LIST. Five plugins over four formats, chosen so that no two claims below
  can be satisfied by the same wrong answer:

    Mangrove   clap  Nassau            2/2  effect      <- same product,
    Mangrove   vst3  Nassau            2/2  effect      <- twice
    Dexed      vst3  DigitalSuburban   0/2  INSTRUMENT
    Castello   au    Luciano           2/2  effect
    Looper     tw    Nassau            2/2  effect

  `vst3` holds two rows of which exactly one is an instrument, so the Format
  and Kind filters compose over a list where neither one alone gives the
  answer; `clap` and `au` hold one row each; and the two Mangroves differ ONLY
  in format, which is the duplicate the ticket is about.

  WHAT PHASE 5 QUIETLY ALSO GATES. `plugins=` is applied to a dialog that has
  already been built, so every dialog here populates TWICE: once over the real
  registry, and once over the list. In phase 5 the stored format is `au`, which
  the real registry does not have — so the first populate cannot restore it and
  falls back to All, and the second one, where `au` exists, has to RECOVER it.
  That is exactly the shape of the browser being opened while the startup scan
  is still running (the registry holds the two built-ins alone, and the scan
  finishes underneath the open dialog), which no case can produce directly:
  a .qxa cannot make a background scan finish mid-dialog. Measured: a build
  whose rebuild keeps the COMBO's value instead of the one the user asked for
  passes every other phase here and fails this one with `format=` where
  `format=au` was expected.
-->
<SActionScript version="1" name="plugin_browser_format_filter">

  <!-- KEY OWNERSHIP. This case OWNS `plugins/browserFormat`, and it is
       RUN_SERIAL for that reason alone (smaragd/CMakeLists.txt): selecting a
       format in the real browser persists the choice into the developer's own
       smaragd.ini. No other case may read or write that key.

       The restore is NOT in this file. `plugins/browserFormat` is a preference
       a developer may have set for themselves rather than a fixture key, and
       settings-remove's header forbids a case removing a key it does not own —
       so `assert-plugin-browser` saves the value and puts it back on every
       path out of its own apply(), including the early return a failed
       assertion takes. Measured both ways on this box: after a passing run
       and after a run whose phase 5 was made to fail, smaragd.ini came back
       byte-identical (md5 feec6e73…, and the key absent before and after). -->


  <setup project="new"/>

  <actions>
    <!-- PHASE 0 — the combo is built from the REGISTRY, not from a table of
         formats this build knows how to host. Over the real registry the only
         guarantee is the in-house format: the pass-through and the native 303
         are linked in and present in every build and every worktree. If the
         combo were populated from a fixed list, `tw` would be there whether or
         not anything carried it, so this is paired with phase 1, where a list
         WITHOUT `tw` must not offer it. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser contains="formats=" absent="formats=|"/>

    <!-- PHASE 1 — every format in the list is offered, sorted, and nothing
         else is. `absent` is the half that bites a fixed table: this list has
         no `lv2` and no `tw`. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx"
        contains="formats=au,clap,vst3|format=|kind=all|filter=|rows=4|visible=4|visibleFormats=au,clap,vst3|status=4 plugin(s)"
        absent="tw"/>

    <!-- PHASE 2 — choosing a format hides the other EXTERNAL formats and keeps
         its own rows and the built-in Looper (QBX-261: the built-ins are
         listed whatever format is chosen). Three of the five, and the status
         line says so: a filter that hid everything and one that hid nothing
         would both fail here, and they fail in opposite directions; the
         pre-QBX-261 filter, which hid Looper too, fails with visible=2. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="vst3"
        contains="format=vst3|kind=all|filter=|rows=5|visible=3|visibleFormats=tw,vst3|status=3 of 5 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 3 — the Format and Kind filters COMPOSE. `vst3` alone gives two
         rows (phase 2) and `instruments` alone gives one row of a different
         format, so only a build that applies both lands on Dexed. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="vst3" kind="instruments"
        contains="format=vst3|kind=instruments|filter=|rows=5|visible=1|visibleFormats=vst3"/>

    <!-- PHASE 4 — ...and so do the Format filter and the typed Filter box,
         which is the pair that reaches the same product in two formats: the
         text matches both Mangroves and the format picks one of them. The
         text goes through the REAL QLineEdit, so this is the production
         textChanged path and not a second reading of what a filter means. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="clap" filter="mangrove"
        contains="format=clap|kind=all|filter=mangrove|rows=5|visible=1|visibleFormats=clap|status=1 of 5 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 5 — THE CHOICE IS REMEMBERED. The first dialog is told `au`,
         which persists it; a SECOND dialog is then built from the same list and
         told nothing, and it must open on `au` by itself. `stored=` reads the
         settings key the dialog writes, which no describe() line can show.

         A note on hermeticity: SOpt::PluginBrowserFormat is a real user
         preference and not a fixture key, so the verb saves and restores it
         around its own work. That is why the round trip happens INSIDE one
         verb rather than across two of them, and why this case can run without
         leaving a developer's browser filtered to something they never chose. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="au" reopen="1" stored="au"
        contains="format=au|kind=all|filter=|rows=5|visible=2|visibleFormats=au,tw|status=2 of 5 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 6 — A STORED FORMAT THIS MACHINE HAS NOTHING IN SHOWS ALL, AND
         IS NOT OVERWRITTEN. The first dialog stores `au`; the reopened one is
         built from a list with no AU in it — the Linux and Windows case for a
         preference carried over from a Mac. It must open on All rather than on
         an empty list, must not offer `au` at all, and must leave the stored
         value alone so the machine that does have AU still honours it.

         The last of those three is what `stored="au"` asserts, and it is the
         one a reasonable implementation gets wrong: restoring the preference
         through the combo would write back whatever the combo fell back to. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="au" reopen="1"
        reopenPlugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        stored="au"
        contains="formats=clap,tw|format=|kind=all|filter=|rows=2|visible=2|visibleFormats=clap,tw|status=2 plugin(s)"
        absent="au"/>

    <!-- PHASE 7 — and the entry is not there to be chosen in the first place.
         `expectSelect="0"` says selecting `vst3` must FAIL over a list that has
         none: the combo offers what exists, so there is no way to ask for an
         empty browser. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="vst3" expectSelect="0"
        contains="formats=clap,tw|format=|kind=all|filter=|rows=2|visible=2|visibleFormats=clap,tw"/>

    <!-- PHASE 8 — All comes back, over the list that carries every format, and
         restores every row. The explicit `all` is also the gesture a user makes
         to undo a filter, and it must persist as EMPTY rather than as the word. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Mangrove:vst3:Nassau:2/2:fx;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx"
        format="all" reopen="1" stored="all"
        contains="format=|kind=all|filter=|rows=5|visible=5|visibleFormats=au,clap,tw,vst3|status=5 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- THE BUILT-INS ARE ALWAYS LISTED (QBX-261). "In all plugin selectors,
         only the plugin type selected is shown. Internal plugins always should
         be listed." The internal plugins are the format `tw`: linked into the
         build, never scanned (the pass-through, the native 303, and what
         registerBuiltin hands in, such as NassauDigital). A Nassau plugin
         shipped as .clap or .vst3 is an installed plugin like any other.

         There is ONE selector, SPluginBrowserDialog, opened from two places:
         the FX strip's "+ Add Effect" on kind effects and its "+ Add
         Instrument" on kind instruments. Phases 9 and 10 build it as each of
         them does. The rule is SPluginBrowserDialog::formatFilterAdmits(),
         the only format test filterPlugins() makes, and visibleFormats= is
         read off the rows that predicate left visible.

         THE LIST. Seven plugins: one built-in effect and one built-in
         instrument, and for each kind one clap and one vst3 external, so
         every phase has an external of ANOTHER format that must stay hidden
         beside the built-in that must stay listed:

           Mangrove   clap  effect        Surge      clap  INSTRUMENT
           Valhalla   vst3  effect        Dexed      vst3  INSTRUMENT
           Looper     tw    effect        Synth303   tw    INSTRUMENT
           Castello   au    effect

         Each `contains` spells visibleFormats= up to the next field, so a
         hidden external showing (`clap,tw,vst3`) fails it as surely as a
         hidden built-in (`clap`). -->

    <!-- PHASE 9 — "+ Add Effect" over CLAP: the clap effect and the built-in
         effect; the vst3 and au effects and every instrument hidden. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Valhalla:vst3:Valhalla:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Surge:clap:Surge:0/2:instrument;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Synth303:tw:Nassau:0/1:instrument"
        kind="effects" format="clap"
        contains="format=clap|kind=effects|filter=|rows=7|visible=2|visibleFormats=clap,tw|status=2 of 7 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 10 — "+ Add Instrument" over VST3: Dexed and the built-in
         instrument; Surge (clap) hidden. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Valhalla:vst3:Valhalla:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Surge:clap:Surge:0/2:instrument;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Synth303:tw:Nassau:0/1:instrument"
        kind="instruments" format="vst3"
        contains="format=vst3|kind=instruments|filter=|rows=7|visible=2|visibleFormats=tw,vst3|status=2 of 7 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 11 — the rule is about the FORMAT filter only. The typed Filter
         box still narrows the built-ins like any row: "mangrove" over CLAP
         leaves Mangrove alone, not Mangrove plus Looper. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Valhalla:vst3:Valhalla:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Surge:clap:Surge:0/2:instrument;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Synth303:tw:Nassau:0/1:instrument"
        kind="all" format="clap" filter="mangrove"
        contains="format=clap|kind=all|filter=mangrove|rows=7|visible=1|visibleFormats=clap|status=1 of 7 plugin(s)"/>

    <!-- PHASE 12 — choosing Built-in itself still narrows, to the built-ins
         alone: the always-listed rule widens an EXTERNAL choice and does not
         turn the tw entry into All. -->
    <assert-plugin-browser
        plugins="Mangrove:clap:Nassau:2/2:fx;Valhalla:vst3:Valhalla:2/2:fx;Looper:tw:Nassau:2/2:fx;Castello:au:Luciano:2/2:fx;Surge:clap:Surge:0/2:instrument;Dexed:vst3:DigitalSuburban:0/2:instrument;Synth303:tw:Nassau:0/1:instrument"
        kind="all" format="tw"
        contains="format=tw|kind=all|filter=|rows=7|visible=2|visibleFormats=tw|status=2 of 7 plugin(s)"/>

  </actions>

  <assertions>
    <!-- Nothing here touches the project: the browser is a dialog over the
         plugin registry. Kept for the same reason media_options_page keeps it,
         as the floor assertion that the run reached the end. -->
    <assert-track-count equals="0"/>
  </assertions>

</SActionScript>
